## 1. Fusion ordering vs the letterless drop — letter+letterless fusion

Traced the implementation (merchants.go:114-126) and confirmed empirically:
a standalone "&" fuses with ANY neighbor pair regardless of whether either
side is letterless — the letterless test only runs after fusion, on the
already-fused token.

- Both-letterless case (SAFE, matches pre-fusion behavior): "STORE 44 & 45"
  -> fuseAmpersands produces "44&45" (letterless) -> dropped whole ->
  Normalize = "STORE". Verified this is IDENTICAL to pre-P10 behavior
  (44, &, 45 would each have been dropped individually too) — no
  regression, confirms the design note in the fuseAmpersands godoc.
  "AT&T STORE 44 & 45" -> "AT&T STORE" (same reasoning, cross-checked).

- Letter+letterless case (REAL, narrow edge case, not a regression against
  P10's stated goal but worth recording): "H & 123" -> "H&123" (survives,
  has letter H) vs "H 123" -> "H" (123 dropped alone). Pre-P10 these both
  normalized to "H" and merged; post-P10 they produce different keys and
  no longer merge. Constructed the realistic variant with a fixed
  cross-street name and a varying leading numeric token: "STORE 44 & MAIN
  ST" -> "STORE 44&MAIN ST" vs "STORE 89 & MAIN ST" -> "STORE 89&MAIN
  ST" — these are two DIFFERENT rich keys (4 tokens each, neither a
  subset of the other since 44&MAIN != 89&MAIN), so two descriptions that
  pre-P10 both collapsed to "STORE MAIN ST" (and merged) now stay split.
  Judged materiality: this requires a letterless token (store/reference
  number) sitting directly to the LEFT of a "&" that is itself followed by
  a stable name/address token, in a source description where that same
  merchant is ALSO written without the leading number+ampersand. This is
  a real but narrow formatting pattern (typical bank descriptors put "&"
  between two name words, e.g. "PROCTER & GAMBLE", "5TH & MAIN", or between
  two reference numbers, e.g. store-number ranges — both of which are
  handled correctly, per above); a bare number immediately before "&
  <name>" is uncommon. No live-data evidence (unlike P9's check-number
  finding, which came from an actual anomaly sweep) and no existing
  fixture triggers it. Non-blocking nit, same tier as the P5 residual this
  task was scoped to close (a real, low-probability coincidence
  requirement, not a systemic bridge).

## 2. Multiple consecutive ampersands

`strings.Fields` never splits "&&" into two tokens (no internal
whitespace), so "A && B" tokenizes as [A, &&, B] — "&&" is a single
letterless token, untouched by fuseAmpersands (exact match on "&" only),
dropped whole -> Normalize("A && B") = "A B". Confirmed empirically.

Genuinely separate ampersands with spaces DO exercise the loop's neighbor
logic: "A & & B" tokenizes as [A, &, &, B]. Traced the loop: at the first
"&" (i=1), the right neighbor check `fields[i+1]` reads fields[2], which
is itself "&" — no special-case, so it fuses literally: out becomes
["A&&"], i is advanced past index 2, and "B" is appended separately.
Result: fuseAmpersands([A,&,&,B]) = [A&&, B] -> Normalize = "A&& B" (not
"A B" like the double-ampersand-joined-token case above). This is a minor
inconsistency (spacing of double ampersands changes the result) but not a
new bridge: "A&&" retains the letter A and both ampersands verbatim, is
just as specific a token as "A" alone would have been, and does not
create any subset-merge opening beyond what "A" already had. No panic or
index-out-of-range: the `i+1 < len(fields)` guard is checked before every
access, and I probed length-1 and trailing/leading unpaired "&" runs
(`[A,&]`, `[&,B]`, `[&]`, `[A,&,&]`, `[&,&,B]`) directly via
fuseAmpersands — all returned cleanly, no panic, in every case.

## 3. Idempotence

Normalize(Normalize(x)) == Normalize(x) checked programmatically across
all ~20 probe cases (including the fused, already-fused-passthrough,
double-ampersand, and edge cases above) — zero violations. Specifically
confirmed the "&" INSIDE a pre-fused token case per the task's own
hypothesis: "BATH & BODY WORKS" -> "BATH&BODY WORKS", and the
already-unspaced "BATH&BODY WORKS" -> "BATH&BODY WORKS" unchanged
(passthrough) — both converge to the identical fixed point, and
fuseAmpersands only ever matches a token that is EXACTLY "&", so an
embedded "&" inside a larger token (e.g. "BATH&BODY") is never touched by
a second pass. Idempotence holds.

